.\" The manual page of tomaccoctl. Keep it in step with tomaccoctl --help:
.\" the test help_and_manual_page_name_the_same_commands_and_flags in
.\" native/main.rs checks that both name the same commands and flags.
.TH TOMACCOCTL 1 "" "Tomacco" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
tomaccoctl \- the Tomacco control plane for building, running and testing mobile apps
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B tomaccoctl call
.I OPERATION
.RI [ JSON " | @" FILE " | @-]"
.RB [ \-\-wait ]
.RB [ \-\-help ]
.br
.B tomaccoctl schema
.RI [ OPERATION " | " \fB\-\-names\fR ]
.br
.B tomaccoctl help
.RI [ OPERATION ]
.br
.B tomaccoctl setup
.RB [ \-\-yes ]
.br
.B tomaccoctl doctor
.br
.B tomaccoctl mcp
.br
.B tomaccoctl replay
.I FILE
.RB [ \-\-dry\-run ]
.br
.B tomaccoctl completions
.BR bash | zsh | fish
.br
.B tomaccoctl update
.RB [ \-\-check ]
.br
.B tomaccoctl uninstall
.br
.B tomaccoctl verify
.I FILE
.RB [ \-\-sig
.IR FILE.sig ]
.RB [ \-\-sha256
.IR FILE.sha256 ]
.RB [ \-\-json ]
.br
.B tomaccoctl report
.RI [ FILE.tar.gz ]
.br
.B tomaccoctl
.BR \-\-version | \-V
.br
.B tomaccoctl
.BR \-\-help | \-h
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B tomaccoctl
builds, runs and tests iOS and Android apps on Linux.
The Tomacco desktop app, this command and its MCP server share one typed
schema of operations, such as
.BR project.run ,
.B device.list
or
.BR ios.virtual.status .
Each operation takes one JSON object of arguments and returns one JSON
envelope:
.B {"ok":true,"result":...}
or
.BR {"ok":false,"error":{"code":...,"message":...,"remedy":...}} .
Long work, such as a build or an installation, runs as a durable job that
outlives the command; see
.BR job.wait ,
.B job.logs
and
.BR job.cancel .
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.BI "call " OPERATION " \fR[\fPJSON\fR | \fP@FILE\fR | \fP@-\fR]\fP"
Run one operation. The arguments are given inline as JSON, read from
.IR FILE ,
or read from standard input with
.BR @- .
Without arguments the operation gets an empty object.
Prints one JSON envelope and exits 0 on success, 1 on failure.
With
.BR \-\-wait ,
an operation that starts a job is followed until the job ends: its log
goes to standard error, the envelope holds the final job record, and only
a succeeded job exits 0.
With
.BR \-\-help ,
describes the operation instead of running it, as
.B help
does.
.TP
.BR schema " [\fIOPERATION\fR | " \-\-names ]
Print every operation with its JSON Schema of arguments, only the named
operation, or with
.B \-\-names
only the names, one per line, as plain text.
.TP
.BR help " [\fIOPERATION\fR]"
Without an operation, print the command-line help. With one, describe it
in plain text: what it does, each argument with its type, choices, limits
and default, whether it changes anything, and an example call.
.TP
.BR setup " [" \-\-yes ]
Check this machine in plain text and offer to install the Android SDK and
connect coding agents.
.B \-\-yes
answers every question with yes.
.TP
.B doctor
Check the toolchains and devices this machine can use, and list what is
left to set up, in order.
.TP
.B mcp
Serve the same operations to a coding agent over standard input and output
(Model Context Protocol). It opens no network port.
.TP
.BR replay " \fIFILE\fR [" \-\-dry\-run ]
Call again, in order, the operations of a session log (see
.BR TOMACCO_SESSION_LOG )
that change something, waiting for each job they start to end. It stops at
the first call that fails. Calls that failed when they were logged, that
only read, that name a job of the logged session or whose arguments were
masked are skipped and listed with the reason.
With
.B \-\-dry\-run
it only lists what it would call.
.TP
.BR completions " " bash | zsh | fish
Print a completion script for the commands, their flags and the operation
names, for example
.BR "source <(tomaccoctl completions bash)" .
.TP
.BR update " [" \-\-check ]
Check for a new release and install it
.RB ( update.check ,
then
.BR update.apply ).
.B \-\-check
only checks.
.TP
.B uninstall
Remove this installation. It keeps your data and the files you changed,
and prints how to undo it.
.TP
.BI "verify " FILE
Check a downloaded release file, such as a package, against its SHA-256
checksum and its detached Ed25519 signature, with the release keys built
into
.BR tomaccoctl :
the current key and, during a key rotation, the next one.
The checksum is read from
.IB FILE .sha256
and the signature from
.IB FILE .sig
unless
.B \-\-sha256
or
.B \-\-sig
names another file. A missing checksum file is reported; a missing
signature fails. Prints a short report, or with
.B \-\-json
one JSON envelope, and exits 0 only when the file is verified.
.TP
.BR report " [" \fIFILE.tar.gz\fR ]
Write a bug-report bundle: version, host, doctor, settings, and recent
failed jobs with the ends of their logs, with secrets masked. Nothing is
sent; read it before you share it.
.SH OPTIONS
These options can stand anywhere on the command line of
.BR call ,
.BR schema ,
.B doctor
and
.BR replay .
.TP
.B \-\-pretty
Indent the JSON. This is the default when standard output is a terminal;
a pipe or a file gets one line.
.TP
.B \-\-compact
One line of JSON, even at a terminal.
.TP
.B \-\-wait
.RB ( call )
Follow a job that the operation starts until it ends.
.TP
.B \-\-names
.RB ( schema )
Print only the operation names.
.TP
.B \-\-dry\-run
.RB ( replay )
List the calls without making them.
.TP
.BR \-\-version ", " \-V
Print the version.
.TP
.BR \-\-help ", " \-h
Print the command-line help.
.SH EXIT STATUS
0 when the command or operation succeeded, 1 when it failed. The error is
in the JSON envelope on standard output.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B TOMACCO_HOME
The data directory: projects, device profiles, job records and logs.
The default is
.IR ~/.local/share/tomacco .
.TP
.B TOMACCO_SESSION_LOG
An absolute file path. Every operation called through
.B call
or
.B mcp
is appended to it as one JSON line: the operation, its arguments with
recognised secrets masked, whether it worked, a short summary of the result
and its duration. Masking recognises known shapes only; read the log before
you share it.
.TP
.B TOMACCO_UPDATES
Set to
.B off
to turn every update check and installation off.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I ~/.local/share/tomacco
Data directory, unless
.B TOMACCO_HOME
names another.
.TP
.I ~/.local/lib/tomacco
The installed programs, tools and operation schema.
.SH EXAMPLES
List the saved project and device workspaces:
.PP
.RS
.B tomaccoctl call workspace.list
.RE
.PP
Describe an operation, then run it:
.PP
.RS
.nf
.B tomaccoctl help project.run
.B tomaccoctl call project.run '{"path":"/home/me/app","platform":"android","device":"emulator-5554"}' \-\-wait
.fi
.RE
.PP
Wait for a job with arguments from standard input:
.PP
.RS
.B echo '{"job_id":"ID","seconds":60}' | tomaccoctl call job.wait @-
.RE
.SH SEE ALSO
The guides are installed in
.I share/doc/tomacco
beside the program, for example
.IR ~/.local/share/doc/tomacco :
.I agents.md
describes the operations and how agents use them,
.I desktop.md
the desktop app.
.PP
Project page: https://github.com/marselnenaj/tomacco
